# Ronin Bridge Hack — Wallet Tracing, Sanctions Attribution, and the Lazarus Group

## Executive Summary

This repository examines the blockchain tracing and attribution process that followed the Ronin Bridge exploit and explores how investigators connected on-chain activity to the North Korean state-sponsored Lazarus Group.

The purpose of this analysis is to examine:

- Blockchain tracing methodologies
- Wallet clustering
- Sanctions attribution
- Exchange exposure
- Mixer usage
- Threat actor attribution
- Cryptocurrency laundering techniques

This case is particularly valuable because it demonstrates how investigators can move from a publicly observable wallet address to a publicly attributed threat actor through the combination of blockchain analysis, intelligence reporting, sanctions investigations, and law enforcement collaboration.

---

# Background

In March 2022, attackers exploited the Ronin Bridge and stole approximately $540 million in cryptocurrency.

The theft became one of the largest cryptocurrency hacks in history.

Following the exploit, investigators began tracing the movement of stolen funds across multiple wallets and services.

One wallet later publicly sanctioned by OFAC was:

```text
0x098B716B8Aaf21512996dC57EB0615e2383E2f96
```

The U.S. Treasury subsequently added the address to the Specially Designated Nationals (SDN) list and publicly associated it with the Ronin Bridge exploit.

Source:

https://ofac.treasury.gov/recent-actions/20220422

